  },
  "grade": "10-12",
  "points": 5,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "The first five triangles in a sequence of right-angled triangles are shown in the figure. If there are 100 triangles in the sequence, how many of the line segments have integer lengths?",
  "image": "qimages/samf/senior-2003-r1/q14.webp",
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "$101$"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "$102$"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "$100$"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "$109$"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "$110$"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "E",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Pythagoras",
    "body": "The first triangle has two sides of length $1$, and each of the other $99$ triangles adds one more side of length $1$: that gives $101$ sides of length $1$. The hypotenuses are $\\sqrt2,\\sqrt3,\\dots,\\sqrt{101}$, since each new hypotenuse squared is the previous one squared plus $1$. Of these, the integers occur for $\\sqrt4,\\sqrt9,\\dots,\\sqrt{100}$, i.e. $2,3,\\dots,10$: nine values. Total $101+9=110$.\n\n**Answer: (E) 110**"
   }
